# Part 3 Notes — Geometry-Based Spatial Graph

## 1. Changes/improvements from previous part

- Replaced the fixed line graph with spatially generated nuclear positions.
- Constructed adjacency from geometry, allowing transport structure to emerge from distance relationships.
- Improved biological realism by allowing irregular spacing between nuclei.

## 2. Key issues with model

- Geometry is still simplified, since nuclei are modelled in one dimension rather than in a full muscle fibre structure.
- Transport strength is still globally controlled and does not yet fully reflect distance-weighted biological exchange.
- Signal production and rescue thresholds remain deterministic and uniform across nuclei.

## 3. Thoughts for next step forward

- Introduce parameter sweeps to test how rescue changes under different transport strengths, thresholds, and corrected fractions.
- Compare clustered and distributed correction patterns.
- Use the next part to shift from building the model structure to analysing model behaviour systematically.
